UNIQUENESS CITATION
-------------------
The precise classical result: any binary LINEAR [24,12,8] code is
equivalent (up to coordinate permutation) to the extended binary Golay
code.  This is due to:

  V. Pless, "On the uniqueness of the Golay codes,"
  Journal of Combinatorial Theory 5 (1968), 215-228.
  (Proves uniqueness of the [23,12,7] and [24,12,8] binary linear codes.)

A Type II (doubly-even self-dual) [24,12] code with d=8 is in particular
a binary linear [24,12,8] code, so Pless (1968) already gives uniqueness;
no Type II hypothesis is needed.  The stronger NONLINEAR statement --
any binary (24, 2^12, 8) code, linear or not, is equivalent to the
extended Golay code -- is due to:

  S. L. Snover, "The uniqueness of the Nordstrom-Robinson and the Golay
  binary codes," Ph.D. thesis, Michigan State University, 1973;
  see also P. Delsarte and J.-M. Goethals, "Unrestricted codes with the
  Golay parameters are unique," Discrete Mathematics 12 (1975), 211-224.

(Within the Type II world: Pless and Sloane, JCTA 18 (1975) 313-335,
classify all nine Type II [24,12] codes; only the Golay code has d=8.)

